There are many functions that are not documented here.  Currently
only slightly over two thirds of the core DeadEnds functions are
listed.

Currently, only functions found in core DeadEnds libraries are listed.
The @code{LLSupport}, @code{Python}, and @code{UI} libraries are
@b{NOT} core DeadEnds libraries.  Descriptions of their functions and
variables will be added in the future.

While the intent is to document @b{ALL} the functions meant to be
called by applications, which functions are in that category isn't
always clear.

In addition to functions meant to be called by applications, there are
functions meant only to be called from within the same library and
functions meant only to be called from within other DeadEnds
libraries.

The hope is that eventually there will be two documents.  One for
applications writers that want to use the library and one for
developers that wish to modify the libraries -- whether to extend them
or to fix bugs.  The second, as yet unwritten, document, will need to
document not just the interfaces, but also the internals.

This document concentrates on the functions in the core DeadEnds
libraries that are called by the port of the LifeLines user interface.
Initially it only included such functions, and a subset at that.  The
document has grown.  It undoubtably only documents some of the
functions that are called by LifeLines, but it also documents many
functions that are not called directly by LifeLines.

It is hoped that it will eventually encompass all of the DeadEnds
functions meant to be called directly by applications.

Additionally, as it is being written for the application developer,
not a library developer, its focus is on the @b{interfaces}, not on
the @b{internals}.  Documentation of the internals is important for
someone wishing to support, maintain, enhance, or extend the
libraries, but is not the focus of this document.  The focus of this
document is on @b{using} the libraries.  Hence the emphasis on the
interfaces.

For the functions which are documented that are @b{NOT} meant to be
called by applications, the hope is that they will eventually become
part of an internals document.
